{"archetypes":[{"name":"marker-contract","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Tracks the in-band scope-reduction marker across detector, collect, dedup, aggregation, tally, and tests where contract drift is likely.","prompt_body":"Investigate whether the plan defines one coherent `[SCOPE-REDUCTION]` contract from reviewer TSV emission through collect, dedup, aggregation, voting, and MainAgent fallback. Check for mismatches between the proposed detector behavior, existing finding block formats, and every caller that must preserve or classify tagged findings. Look for places where tagged OOS blocks, severity prefixes, aggregation fallback, or duplicate merging could accidentally change semantics beyond the plan’s stated intent. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"anchor-threading","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"Verifies the staged issue anchor is consistently built, sanitized, propagated, and not confused with brainstorm context or prior plans.","prompt_body":"Trace the proposed `SCOPE_ANCHOR_FILE` lifecycle from feature resolution through scout, panel, voters, tally, revise, Step 3 result env, and MainAgent re-tally. Compare the plan against repository scripts to find any missing argument plumbing, stale context path, allowlist gap, or unsafe env emission path. Pay special attention to the distinction between binding issue scope, approved outline, brainstorm context, and stripped prior `larch:plan` content. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"regression-matrix","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"Checks whether the large proposed harness expansion maps cleanly to existing test structure without redundant or missing coverage.","prompt_body":"Audit the proposed test additions against the existing harness layout and Makefile targets to identify missing regression cases, duplicate coverage, or tests assigned to the wrong harness. Verify that default-compatibility checks, unchanged vote thresholds, marker-loss fallbacks, and prompt-framing assertions are covered at the right integration level. Look for any acceptance criterion that lacks a concrete harness path or any new harness that may not be invoked by lint. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
